<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuringian Highlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thuringian Slate Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thuringian Highlands are an upland region of slate hills in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> with the Franconian Forest. Poor in farmland but rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and slate, the area was divided among small lordships, notably the many lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>, whose courts sat at towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lobenstein">Lobenstein</a> and Ebersdorf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuringian-highlands.jpg" alt="Thuringian Highlands" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schieferhaus_Gehren.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
